The code for handling single-choice HQL report parameters (e.g., SUBJECT or DEPARTMENT) unconditionally processed the parameter value returned by `helper.getParameter(option.name())`, without checking whether it was `null`. When a single-choice parameter was not actually selected/provided by the user, this method returned `null`, but the code still attempted to look up or parse this `null` value (via `option.lookupValue()` and `Long.valueOf()`), which could throw a `NullPointerException` or otherwise produce an invalid/empty `IdValue` entry added to the `params` list. This caused the CSV export process to fail whenever a report contained such a parameter without an explicit value.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
`String value = helper.getParameter(option.name());`
`Long id = option.lookupValue(context.getUser(), value);`
`id = Long.valueOf(value);`
Reason: The code retrieves the parameter value for a single-choice option without checking if it is `null`. When the parameter is not actually provided by the user, `helper.getParameter(option.name())` returns `null`, but this `null` value is still passed to `option.lookupValue()` and potentially to `Long.valueOf()`, which can throw a `NullPointerException` or produce invalid results, causing the CSV export to fail.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
`if (value != null) {`
`Long id = option.lookupValue(context.getUser(), value);`
`params.add(v);`
Reason: By wrapping the parameter processing logic inside a `null` check (`if (value != null)`), the code now skips creating and adding an `IdValue` entry when the parameter value is `null`, preventing the `NullPointerException` and avoiding adding invalid/empty parameter entries to the `params` list, thus allowing the CSV export to complete successfully even when the single-choice parameter is not explicitly provided.


